//! The SOS call numbers — KERNEL-INTERNAL (design 140; sos/spec.md §5.7).
//!
//! Every op number, rights bit, status tag and handle type in the system, in
//! one place.
//! Exactly two things import this: the kernel's dispatch tables
//! (`sos/kernel/core/`) and the `sos` wrapper module the kernel package exports
//! to userspace (`sos/kernel/sysapi/`). Nothing else, ever.
//!
//! WHY IT IS SHARED RATHER THAN DUPLICATED: the dispatch and the wrappers are
//! the two halves of one contract. Held apart, they skew — and a syscall-number
//! skew is not a compile error anywhere, it is a working call that does the
//! wrong thing. Held together, renumbering is a one-line edit that either
//! rebuilds both halves or fails.
//!
//! WHY IT IS KERNEL-INTERNAL: the Zircon-vDSO discipline (ratified Aug 5).
//! NUMBERS ARE NOT ABI. Userspace links the exported `sos` module and calls
//! methods; it never writes `7` for an op, so the kernel may renumber freely
//! between builds. The one thing a process must agree with the kernel about is
//! the CALLING CONVENTION (§5.7: handle in a0, op in a7), and that lives in the
//! per-arch HAL stub, not here.
//!
//! WHY THESE ARE ENUMS AND NOT STATICS (design 145/153). Every family here is a
//! CLOSED NAMED SET of values, which is what an Int-inheriting enum is for: it
//! names the set, PINS the numbers so a reorder cannot renumber them, gives the
//! dispatch an exhaustive `match`, and turns the read at the trap boundary into
//! `from(raw:)` — a total function returning `E?`, so an unrecognized number is
//! DATA the kernel rejects rather than an index it trusts. The parallel statics
//! this replaced (`OP_SYSTEM_DEBUG_PRINT`, `SYS_ERR_BAD_OP`, …) could be
//! compared against anything and validated only by a hand-written `> MAX` test.
//!
//! HOW A FAMILY IS NAMED (ruled Aug 15, user). An op table or a rights set is
//! named for the spec §2 OBJECT it belongs to, spelled OUT, plus the
//! term-of-art suffix §5.7 already uses: `SystemOp`, `ProcessOp`, `ThreadOp`,
//! `EventOp`, `WaiterOp`, `SystemRight`, `ProcessRight`, `ThreadRight`,
//! `EventRight`, `WaiterRight`. No abbreviated object prefix — not `SysOp`, not
//! `ProcRight`, not `EvtRight` — because the abbreviation saves four characters
//! in a file nobody types into and costs every reader the question of whether
//! `Sys` means the System object or the syscall. The rule extends to anything
//! else here named after an object (`ProcessStatusKind`, `process_status_word`,
//! `EventMode`, `WaiterReadiness`), and the next object kind inherits it: name
//! the family after the row in the §2 table.
//!
//! HOW AN OP IS NAMED (ruled Aug 17, user). Inside a family an op reads
//! `object_operation`: the OBJECT KIND the op concerns leads, then the verb —
//! `ProcessOp.ThreadCreate`, `SystemOp.ClockGet`, `ClockOp.TimerCreate` — and
//! the typed method and the C export mirror it (`process.thread_create`,
//! `sos_system_clock_get`). An op whose RECEIVER IS the object it concerns keeps
//! the bare verb, there being no second noun to lead with: `Arm`, `Ack`, `Wait`,
//! `Signal`, `Join`, `Start`, `Exit`, `Yield`, `Now`, `Add`, `Remove`,
//! `Shutdown`, `DebugPrint`, `GetStatus`. What the rule buys is that every op
//! concerning a kind sorts together — a reader looking for what makes a Timer
//! finds `TimerCreate` without having to know which object is the factory — and
//! that a rights bit named after an op (`ProcessRight.ThreadCreate`) reads
//! exactly like the op it gates. A right naming a CAPABILITY rather than an op
//! (`Transfer`, `Manage`, `Read`, `Control`, `Attach`, `Debug`) is not under this
//! rule and keeps its own noun.
//!
//! Delivery in v1 is static linking through the manifest/module-path
//! mechanism. A genuinely mapped vDSO is an object-model-era upgrade that
//! changes none of the above.

// =============================================================================
// Object types
// =============================================================================

/// What a handle-table entry names.
///
/// `Unbound` is the reserved zero: handle 0 names nothing, so a zeroed register
/// can never be mistaken for authority, and a table slot that was never filled
/// reads as "no object" rather than as object type 0.
///
/// A `UInt8` backing because this is a kernel-internal field in `HandleEntry`,
/// never a number that crosses the trap boundary.
public enum ObjType: UInt8 {
    case Unbound = 0,
    case System = 1,
    case Process = 2,
    case Thread = 3,
    case Event = 4,
    case Waiter = 5,
    case Interrupt = 6,
    case Clock = 7,
    case Timer = 8,
}

// WHICH KINDS ARE WAITABLE is deliberately NOT recorded here (§2.2). It cannot
// be a property of the kind alone: attaching a handle to a Waiter also spends a
// RIGHT, and which enum's bits that right lives in is decided by the kind — so
// the two questions are one question, answered in one exhaustive `match` in the
// kernel's `waitable_slot`. A `Bool` here would be the same fact in a second
// place, and the place that could go stale.

// =============================================================================
// Handles (spec §3)
// =============================================================================

/// A handle naming the kernel's System object.
///
/// Each object kind gets its OWN handle type (ratified Aug 7). Saw's distinct
/// `type` alias gives exactly the asymmetry a handle wants for free: it flows
/// TO `UInt` implicitly, so the syscall stub takes it with no cast and no
/// conversion is emitted — but a raw word, or another kind's handle, cannot
/// flow IN. Crossing into a handle type is an explicit construction, and there
/// are only two places that do it: userspace adopting its boot handle, and the
/// kernel's dispatch AFTER the table has resolved the handle to a System
/// object. That is what makes the type mean "validated as System" rather than
/// merely "a number".
///
/// The typing stops at the ABI boundary. The `@export`ed vDSO symbols and the
/// `sos_syscallN` stubs keep raw `UInt` words, because a C caller sees words
/// and the export whitelist is primitives; the handle TABLE indexes by word too.
///
/// This is TIER ONE of two — kind safety. When M2 brings closeable and
/// transferable handles, the OWNING tier is a `NoCopy` struct wrapping this
/// alias (deinit closes, `move` transfers — the `TcpStream` pattern, and §3's
/// no-DUPLICATE rule is its exact `NoCopy` correspondence). That is additive:
/// this alias becomes the payload, and nothing written against it changes.
public type SystemHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Process object (§2 table). Same discipline as
/// `SystemHandle`: it flows TO `UInt` at the syscall stub and nothing flows in.
public type ProcessHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Thread object (§2 table).
public type ThreadHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming an Event object (§2.4). Same discipline again.
public type EventHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Waiter object (§2.2).
public type WaiterHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming an Interrupt object (§2 table, §9) — an IRQ line bound to a
/// waitable. Same discipline as every other handle type.
public type InterruptHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Clock object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1) — a granted
/// time source. Same discipline as every other handle type.
public type ClockHandle = UInt

/// A handle naming a Timer object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1) — a deadline
/// bound to the Clock that created it. Same discipline again.
public type TimerHandle = UInt

/// A handle index the table can never hand out. Handle 0 names nothing, so a
/// zeroed register is never authority (see `ObjType.Unbound`).
public static NO_HANDLE: UInt = 0

/// The `entry` argument to `Process.ThreadCreate` that means "start the thread
/// at this process's own IMAGE ENTRY" — the entry the loader took out of the
/// image header.
///
/// It exists because of a LANGUAGE GAP, and the gap is worth stating where the
/// constant is: Saw cannot name the address of a function (design 172's reason
/// 3, DF-172a — `extern` declares functions, an extern function is not usable
/// as a value, and `@export` on a static emits a definition). So a process
/// written in Saw has no way to compute an entry address for a thread it wants
/// to create, and this is the one entry it can ask for by name. A process that
/// CAN produce an address — one with a C leaf, which is what the harness's
/// thread images use — passes it and the kernel checks it against the image's
/// granted region. See DF-178c.
public static ENTRY_IMAGE: UInt = 0

// =============================================================================
// Rights (spec §3)
// =============================================================================

// RIGHTS ARE SCOPED PER OBJECT KIND (ratified Aug 7). Each kind declares its
// own rights enum, so a bit means nothing until you know which kind's word it
// sits in — and dispatch establishes the kind before any right is checked (§3's
// order: table lookup -> object type -> op table -> rights check -> op). Two
// consequences worth stating plainly:
//
//   - Kind-specific bits OVERLAP freely across kinds. A rights word is only ever
//     interpreted against its handle's kind, so each kind owns 24 bits of its
//     own instead of all kinds sharing 32.
//   - The check is TYPED. `SystemObject.allows` takes a `SystemRight` and
//     nothing else, so testing a channel right against a System handle is a
//     compile error rather than a bit comparison that quietly answers.
//
// THE UNIVERSAL LOW BYTE. Bits 0-7 are identical in EVERY kind's enum:
//
//   bit 0   Transfer   may be sent over a channel — the movability capability
//   bit 1   Manage     derive children
//   bits 2-7           RESERVED (candidates as they prove universal:
//                      wait/signal, introspect/info, revoke)
//
// Six bits are reserved rather than assigned because more shared operations are
// coming and a universal right added later must not have to move a kind's bits
// to make room. Generic kernel paths — channel handle-transfer is the first —
// check universal bits without knowing the kind, which is only sound while the
// table holds, so the `static_assert`s below hold it: they are what stops a kind
// enum from drifting, and what catches a kind-specific right placed below bit 8.

/// A right on the System object.
///
/// The low byte is the universal table above; System's own rights start at bit
/// 8. `Debug` gates the console, `Shutdown` gates stopping the machine — a
/// process holding the handle without them can name the object and do nothing
/// with it, which is the point of checking rights per-OP rather than per-object.
/// `ClockGet` gates the one op that hands out a time source, and it is a right
/// rather than an ambient facility for a reason worth stating where the bit is
/// (design 232 unit 1; the M3 time notes): TIME IS A GRANTED CAPABILITY. A
/// launcher that strips this bit from a child's System handle has a process
/// that cannot read the machine's clock at all — which is what makes handing it
/// a VIRTUAL clock over IPC later a substitution rather than a rewrite. It is
/// deliberately NOT in the stripped-by-construction set the M3 device rulings
/// describe (`Shutdown` and the bind/alloc ops): an ordinary process needs to
/// tell the time, and a clock grants authority over nothing.
public enum SystemRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Debug = 256,
    case Shutdown = 512,
    case ClockGet = 1024,
}

// The universal low byte, pinned. Every kind's enum repeats these two asserts
// verbatim; that repetition IS the check, since it is what makes a drifted bit
// fail to build rather than fail in a channel transfer years later.
static_assert((SystemRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((SystemRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")

// Kind-specific rights start at bit 8, leaving bits 2-7 free for universal
// rights not yet assigned. One assert per kind-specific case.
static_assert((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Debug must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Shutdown must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right ClockGet must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Process object (§2 table row; design 178 D4).
///
/// `Manage` is the universal derive bit and here it means one thing: asking the
/// process for the CALLING THREAD's handle. That is a derivation in the §3
/// sense — a handle obtained through a handle already held — and it is how a
/// thread reaches its own Thread object without the kernel handing out ambient
/// authority.
///
/// `Kill` is deliberately ABSENT. Spec §8 ratifies it as the capability-gated
/// escape hatch userspace supervision needs against a wedged process — ANOTHER
/// process. With one process in the system it is `Exit` under a second name,
/// and an op whose only reachable use is degenerate is an op nothing tests. It
/// lands with M3's multi-process loading, beside `create_process`.
///
/// `EventCreate` and `WaiterCreate` are M2 unit 3's, and where they sit ANSWERS
/// an open pin: spec §12 leaves the creation-authority model for plain objects
/// (Channel/Event/Waiter/Timer) open between quota-gated free creation and a
/// factory capability. This is the factory capability, and it is one a process
/// already holds — the Process object is what an Event or a Waiter is CHARGED
/// to (its slab slot, its handle-table entry, its teardown), so making the
/// authority to create one flow through the Process handle is §3's derivation
/// rule rather than a new mechanism beside it. A quota is additive on top: it
/// would be a field on the process slot, checked where `NoResource` is returned
/// today. Nothing here forecloses it.
///
/// ONE BIT PER CREATION OP rather than a single `Create`, matching
/// `ThreadCreate`. The bits are what attenuation strips, so an object kind a
/// process may not make is a bit it was not given — collapsing them would make
/// "may create Events" and "may create Waiters" the same capability, which they
/// are not. Twenty-four bits are available and six are used.
///
/// `InterruptBind` is the one whose absence MATTERS today, and it is worth
/// saying why the same shape carries a much stronger authority: an Interrupt
/// BINDS AN IRQ LINE, so the bit is what separates a driver process from an
/// ordinary one. In M2 root holds it because root is the only process there is;
/// M3's launcher strips it from everything it spawns except the drivers, which
/// is §3's monotonic attenuation doing exactly what it is for.
public enum ProcessRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case ThreadCreate = 256,
    case Exit = 512,
    case Wait = 1024,
    case EventCreate = 2048,
    case WaiterCreate = 4096,
    case InterruptBind = 8192,
}

static_assert((ProcessRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right ThreadCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Exit must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right EventCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right WaiterCreate must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ProcessRight.InterruptBind as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right InterruptBind must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Thread object (§2 table row; design 178 D4).
///
/// The split is between what an OWNER of a thread may do to it — `Start` it,
/// `Join` it — and what a thread does to ITSELF, which is `Control`: exit, and
/// yield the processor. Both of the latter check that the caller IS the named
/// thread, so `Control` on someone else's handle still cannot drive it; the
/// right is what makes the check worth running rather than the check itself.
public enum ThreadRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Start = 256,
    case Join = 512,
    case Control = 1024,
}

static_assert((ThreadRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Start as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Start must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Join as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Join must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ThreadRight.Control as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Control must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Event object (§2.4; design 178 D5).
///
/// THE THREE ARE THE THREE ENDS OF A NOTIFICATION and they attenuate apart on
/// purpose. `Signal` is the producer's — it is the whole of what a driver or a
/// peer needs, and a handle carrying only this bit can raise the event and can
/// never learn anything from it. `Receive` is the consumer's: it drains the
/// word and resets it. `Wait` is the right to have READINESS OBSERVED — what
/// attaching to a Waiter spends — and it is separate from `Receive` because a
/// dispatcher that parks on a set of events need not be the code that reads any
/// of them.
///
/// `Wait` IS A CONSUMING RIGHT TOO, since the Aug-17 ruling: a wait's record
/// carries the accumulated word and the delivery clears it, so a handle holding
/// only this bit takes values as well as observes them. That was always half
/// true — the record has carried the word since M2 — and what changed is that it
/// now empties the object as well. The two bits therefore attenuate by DOOR
/// (park or poll) rather than by whether the holder learns anything, and a
/// launcher that wants a pure readiness observer has no bit for it. Nothing in
/// SOS needs one yet: with no handle transfer before M4, every right on an Event
/// is held by the process that made it.
///
/// `Manage` is withheld at creation for the reason `thread_rights` gives: an
/// Event derives nothing, so the universal derive bit would name an authority
/// that does not exist.
public enum EventRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Signal = 256,
    case Receive = 512,
    case Wait = 1024,
}

static_assert((EventRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((EventRight.Signal as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Signal must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Receive as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Receive must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((EventRight.Wait as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Waiter object (§2.2; design 178 D5).
///
/// `Attach` gates BOTH `Add` and `Remove`, which is one bit for what reads like
/// two operations — deliberately, because they are one capability: the power to
/// decide what a Waiter watches. A holder who may add may already replace the
/// whole attachment set by adding and never removing, so splitting the pair
/// would produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to PARK on it, and it is separate because the two roles
/// genuinely divide: §2.2 ratifies many threads waiting on one Waiter, so a
/// pool of workers holds `Wait` while whoever builds the attachment set holds
/// `Attach`.
public enum WaiterRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Attach = 256,
    case Wait = 512,
}

static_assert((WaiterRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Attach must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on an Interrupt object (§2 table; spec §9; design 178 D6).
///
/// TWO RIGHTS, AND THEY ARE THE TWO HALVES OF spec §9's CYCLE. `Wait` is the
/// right to have the line's assertion OBSERVED — what attaching to a Waiter
/// spends, the same meaning `EventRight.Wait` has — and `Ack` is the right to
/// END a fire, which is what unmasks the line and lets the device interrupt
/// again. They attenuate apart for a real shape: a supervisor that watches a
/// driver's lines holds `Wait` alone and can never rearm one, which is exactly
/// the authority a monitor should have.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Mask` right and no mask op. Masking is the
/// KERNEL's half of the cycle (§9's mask-on-fire), not something a driver
/// asks for — a driver that could mask a line at will could also leave it
/// masked, which is a denial of the device with no diagnostic. What a driver
/// controls is its own device's interrupt-enable register, through the window
/// it was granted, which is where that decision belongs.
///
/// `Manage` is withheld at creation for the reason `thread_rights` gives: an
/// Interrupt derives nothing, so the universal derive bit would name an
/// authority that does not exist.
public enum InterruptRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Wait = 256,
    case Ack = 512,
}

static_assert((InterruptRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Ack must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Clock object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1).
///
/// THE TWO SPLIT READING TIME FROM SCHEDULING IT, and they attenuate apart for
/// a shape that already exists: a process that only ever stamps a log line
/// holds `Read` alone and can never consume a Timer slot, while a dispatcher
/// that arms timeouts holds both. Neither is the other's subset in practice —
/// `TimerCreate` without `Read` is a process that sleeps by the clock without
/// being able to name what time it is, which is exactly what a pure event loop
/// does.
///
/// `Manage` is withheld at creation for the reason `ProcessRight` gives about
/// one bit per creation op: a Clock's only derivation IS a Timer, and that has
/// its own bit, so the universal derive bit would be a second name for it.
public enum ClockRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Read = 256,
    case TimerCreate = 512,
}

static_assert((ClockRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Read must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right TimerCreate must start at bit 8 or above")

/// A right on a Timer object (§2 table; design 232 unit 1).
///
/// `Arm` GATES BOTH `Arm` AND `Disarm`, which is one bit for what reads like two
/// operations — the same call `WaiterRight.Attach` makes, and for the same
/// reason: they are one capability, the power to decide when this timer fires.
/// A holder who may arm can already silence a timer by arming it a century out,
/// so splitting the pair would produce a right that withholds nothing.
///
/// `Wait` is the right to have readiness OBSERVED — what attaching to a Waiter
/// spends, the same meaning it has on an Event and an Interrupt — and it is
/// separate because the roles genuinely divide: a supervisor parked on a set of
/// timeouts holds `Wait` alone and can never reschedule one.
///
/// `Manage` is withheld at creation for the reason `thread_rights` gives: a
/// Timer derives nothing, so the universal derive bit would name an authority
/// that does not exist.
public enum TimerRight: UInt32 {
    case Transfer = 1,
    case Manage = 2,
    case Arm = 256,
    case Wait = 512,
}

static_assert((TimerRight.Transfer as UInt32) == 1,
              "universal right Transfer must be bit 0 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Manage as UInt32) == 2,
              "universal right Manage must be bit 1 in every kind's enum")
static_assert((TimerRight.Arm as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Arm must start at bit 8 or above")
static_assert((TimerRight.Wait as UInt32) >= 256,
              "kind-specific right Wait must start at bit 8 or above")

/// What the kernel mints for root's System handle at boot (§12).
///
/// A function rather than a `static` because a static initializer takes plain
/// literals only — and `768` with a comment saying which bits it is made of is
/// exactly the magic number this file is getting rid of.
///
/// Root gets the two System rights plus `Manage`, and not `Transfer`: there is
/// nobody to transfer a handle to in a one-process world. `Manage` is new in
/// M2 and it is what makes the boot set a SEED rather than the whole of root's
/// authority — root asks the System object for its own Process handle, and the
/// Process object for its own Thread handle, so every later object arrives
/// through a handle already held (§3's derivation rule) instead of through a
/// register the kernel had to widen.
///
/// Attenuation is monotonic, so a right withheld here can never appear later —
/// which is the right default for a set the boot handle hands out.
public func root_system_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((SystemRight.Debug as UInt32) | (SystemRight.Shutdown as UInt32)
     | (SystemRight.Manage as UInt32) | (SystemRight.ClockGet as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for root's own Process handle (§12, extended by
/// design 178 D4).
///
/// Everything the Process object can do in M2, because root IS the system: make
/// threads, hand a thread its own handle, exit, read its own status, and make
/// every object kind there is — Interrupts included, since root is also the
/// only process there is to drive a device.
///
/// `InterruptBind` is the bit M3 will stop handing out freely: root keeps it
/// (it launches the drivers), and everything root spawns that is not one gets a
/// handle without it, which is §3's monotonic attenuation with nothing new
/// added to make it work.
public func root_process_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ProcessRight.Manage as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.ThreadCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.Exit as UInt32) | (ProcessRight.Wait as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.EventCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.WaiterCreate as UInt32)
     | (ProcessRight.InterruptBind as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a thread's own handle — root's initial thread at
/// boot, and every thread `Process.ThreadCreate` makes.
///
/// `Manage` is withheld: a Thread object derives nothing, so the bit would name
/// an authority that does not exist. The three that are here are the whole of
/// the object's surface, which is deliberate — a thread's creator holds its
/// handle and so does the thread itself (one handle, §3's no-duplicate rule),
/// so an attenuated split between the two would need two handles.
public func thread_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ThreadRight.Start as UInt32) | (ThreadRight.Join as UInt32)
     | (ThreadRight.Control as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Event a process has just created (§2.4).
///
/// The creator gets every end of its own notification — it made the object, so
/// there is nobody to attenuate against yet. Attenuation happens when a handle
/// is DERIVED for somebody else, and §3 makes that monotonic, so a producer
/// handed `Signal` alone can never widen back to this.
public func event_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((EventRight.Signal as UInt32) | (EventRight.Receive as UInt32)
     | (EventRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Waiter a process has just created (§2.2).
public func waiter_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((WaiterRight.Attach as UInt32) | (WaiterRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for an Interrupt a process has just bound (§9).
///
/// Both ends of the cycle, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the creator
/// made the object and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. A monitor
/// handle carrying `Wait` alone is a DERIVATION, and §3 makes derivation
/// monotonic, so it can never widen back to this.
public func interrupt_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((InterruptRight.Wait as UInt32) | (InterruptRight.Ack as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Clock a process has just been granted (§2 table).
///
/// Both ends, for the reason `event_rights` gives: the grant is the whole
/// capability and there is nobody to attenuate against yet. A child handed a
/// read-only clock is a DERIVATION, and §3 makes derivation monotonic, so it
/// can never widen back to this.
public func clock_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((ClockRight.Read as UInt32) | (ClockRight.TimerCreate as UInt32))
}

/// What the kernel mints for a Timer a process has just created (§2 table).
public func timer_rights() -> UInt32 {
    ((TimerRight.Arm as UInt32) | (TimerRight.Wait as UInt32))
}

// =============================================================================
// The System object's op table (spec §2)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the System object — not a global syscall number. Another
/// object type numbers its own ops from zero, and the pair (handle, op) is what
/// selects an implementation.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the op arrives in (a7), because
/// that value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED. A narrower backing would need the kernel to
/// truncate a7 before `from(raw:)` could look at it, and `0x100` would then
/// arrive as a valid `DebugPrint` — a hostile process picking an op it was
/// refused. At register width the conversion is exact and `from(raw:)` rejects
/// every number that is not an op, which is also what retired `OP_SYSTEM_MAX`:
/// the range check and the decode are now one operation instead of two that
/// could disagree.
/// `ClockGet` is the fourth, and it is a GETTER rather than a factory: it
/// answers with the process's Clock for the domain asked about, minting the
/// object on first ask and handing back the SAME handle every time after — the
/// shape `ProcessSelf` above already has. There is one monotonic clock on a
/// machine, so two handles to it would be two names for one thing, which §3's
/// no-duplicate rule exists to prevent; and an op that cannot allocate on
/// repetition needs no quota row when quotas land.
public enum SystemOp: UInt {
    case DebugPrint = 0,
    case Shutdown = 1,
    case ProcessSelf = 2,
    case ClockGet = 3,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Process object's op table (spec §2, §7, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the Process object. Numbered from zero in its OWN table —
/// `ThreadCreate` is 0 here and `DebugPrint` is 0 on System, and the pair
/// (handle, op) is what tells them apart.
///
/// `ThreadCreate` lives here rather than on Thread because there is no Thread
/// to name yet, and because spec §7 puts the band map on the Process object:
/// thread creation is where a band is resolved against it, which makes the
/// Process the enforcement point. (M2 resolves nothing — D3 keeps the map a
/// loader artifact — but the op is already in the place that will.)
///
/// Absent, with reasons: `Kill` (see `ProcessRight`), and `CreateProcess`,
/// which needs an image to load and a second address space to load it into —
/// both M3, per design 178's option A.
public enum ProcessOp: UInt {
    case ThreadCreate = 0,
    case ThreadSelf = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case GetStatus = 3,
    case EventCreate = 4,
    case WaiterCreate = 5,
    case InterruptBind = 6,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Thread object's op table (spec §2, §8; design 178 D4)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on the Thread object.
///
/// `Join` is here because design 178's D4 puts it here (ratified Aug 15), which
/// REVISES spec §8's "no join syscall" (ratified Aug 3). The §8 reasoning was
/// that threads are pool workers and work completion is a task-level concept;
/// D4 keeps the object honest instead — a thread you can create and start is a
/// thread whose completion you must be able to observe, and §8 already conceded
/// the observation is needed (it proposed thread handles as WAITABLES). Join is
/// the same observation without a Waiter, which is the next unit's object. When
/// Waiter lands, `Join` becomes the one-thread shorthand for parking on it.
public enum ThreadOp: UInt {
    case Start = 0,
    case Join = 1,
    case Exit = 2,
    case Yield = 3,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Event object's op table (spec §2.4; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on an Event object.
///
/// TWO OPS AND THE OBJECT IS COMPLETE, which is why §2.4 calls it the smallest
/// thing that can be a kernel object at all: bits go IN and never block, and one
/// read takes them all OUT and resets. There is no third question to ask an
/// Event — "is it ready" is `word != 0`, and it is asked by the Waiter, not by
/// an op.
///
/// `Receive` IS THE NON-BLOCKING DOOR, not the only one: a `Waiter.wait`
/// delivery takes the same word the same way (ruled Aug 17, user). What the two
/// differ in is parking, which is why this stayed one op rather than growing a
/// blocking twin.
///
/// There is deliberately no non-draining PEEK. A drain that could be skipped
/// would make level-triggered readiness a thing a reader could observe without
/// clearing, so a poll loop could spin forever on a readiness nothing consumes;
/// §2.4's "one drain reads-and-clears" is what forecloses that, and one op is
/// how it stays foreclosed.
public enum EventOp: UInt {
    case Signal = 0,
    case Receive = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Waiter object's op table (spec §2.2; design 178 D5)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Waiter object.
///
/// The names are §2.2's own (`add`, `remove`, `wait`); the RIGHT that gates the
/// first two is `WaiterRight.Attach`, which is one capability under a name that
/// says what it is rather than repeating either op.
///
/// **`Remove` TAKES THE KEY, NOT THE WAITABLE'S HANDLE** (ruled Aug 16, user).
/// Detaching edits the WAITER's own attachment table and never touches the
/// waitable at all, so the authority it needs is the Waiter handle already in
/// the handle register plus the key that names the attachment — asking for the
/// waitable's handle too would be asking for authority the operation does not
/// spend. It also has to be this way for M3: once handles can be CLOSED, a
/// closed waitable's stale attachment is one whose handle no longer resolves,
/// and a handle-based remove could never name it.
///
/// THE INVARIANT THAT FORCES: **keys are UNIQUE per Waiter.** `Add` with a key
/// the Waiter already uses is a FAULT — caller-checkable, since the attacher
/// chose both keys — and it has to be refused rather than allowed, because a
/// duplicate makes two different questions ambiguous at once: which attachment
/// a `Remove` names, and which one a `Wait` answer came from.
public enum WaiterOp: UInt {
    case Add = 0,
    case Remove = 1,
    case Wait = 2,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Interrupt object's op table (spec §2 table, §9; design 178 D6)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on an Interrupt object.
///
/// ONE OP, and the object is complete — which is the same claim `EventOp` makes
/// and for a sharper reason. Everything an Interrupt does TO a process happens
/// through a Waiter (§2.2), and everything the KERNEL does to the line happens
/// in the trap path; what is left for the holder to ask is the one question
/// spec §9 names: this fire is finished, let the device interrupt again.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Wait` op here. Parking is the Waiter's, and a
/// second way to park on one Interrupt would be a second wake protocol to keep
/// in step with the first — §2.2's aggregator exists precisely so that every
/// waitable has exactly one.
///
/// There is deliberately no `Bind`/`Unbind` pair either: the binding IS the
/// object's existence (`ProcessOp.InterruptBind` takes the line), so a bound
/// line is a live slot and an unbound one is a slot that was freed. A rebindable
/// Interrupt would let one handle name two different devices over its life,
/// which is exactly what a capability must not do.
public enum InterruptOp: UInt {
    case Ack = 0,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Clock object's op table (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Clock object.
///
/// TWO OPS: read the time, and make something that fires against it. There is
/// deliberately no SET — a Monotonic clock that could be set would not be one,
/// and the domains that CAN step (Realtime) are future values of `ClockType`
/// that will bring their own op if they need it.
///
/// `Now` answers through a COPY-OUT RECORD rather than the value register,
/// because a nanosecond count is 64 bits and one of the two profiles has 32-bit
/// registers. The vDSO true-mapping upgrade (spec §5.7) is the named
/// syscall-free future for this one op and changes nothing about its shape.
public enum ClockOp: UInt {
    case Now = 0,
    case TimerCreate = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Timer object's op table (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================

/// A METHOD ID on a Timer object.
///
/// TWO OPS, AND NO ACK — which is the difference from an Interrupt worth reading
/// here. §9's cycle needs an ack because the KERNEL masks the line and only the
/// driver can say the device has been serviced; a Timer has no device and no
/// mask, so it re-arms itself and a fire is consumed by the WAIT that reports
/// it. That is the timerfd model, and it is what makes a periodic timer a
/// fire-and-forget schedule rather than a request/response.
///
/// `Arm` takes its arguments through a COPY-IN RECORD, because two 64-bit
/// nanosecond counts are four words on a 32-bit profile and the argument
/// registers are three. See `TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS` below.
///
/// `Disarm` is separate from "arm with no deadline" because the two mean
/// different things to the fire count: arming REPLACES a schedule and clears
/// what the old one owed, while disarming says the timer is off and owes
/// nothing at all. Collapsing them would make "cancel" and "reschedule"
/// indistinguishable at the ABI.
public enum TimerOp: UInt {
    case Arm = 0,
    case Disarm = 1,
}

/// WHICH CLOCK a Timer runs against, and what `System.ClockGet` names (§2's
/// Clock row; design 232 unit 1, the M3 time notes).
///
/// **v1 DECLARES `Monotonic` AND NOTHING ELSE.** `Boot` and `Realtime` are named
/// in the design as future domains and are deliberately NOT declared here: an
/// unproducible case is dead surface, and a caller able to name one the kernel
/// cannot mint would get a runtime refusal where the absence is a compile error.
/// The raw backing is what lets them be ADDED without renumbering (design 145's
/// wire idiom), which is the whole point of stating the number.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the domain arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` and `EventMode` give: the value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED, so a
/// narrower backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100`
/// would arrive as a valid `Monotonic`.
///
/// MONOTONIC'S CONTRACT: nanoseconds since the machine started counting, never
/// stepping and never going backwards. The epoch is not published and must not
/// be relied on — two readings are comparable, one reading means nothing on its
/// own.
public enum ClockType: UInt {
    case Monotonic = 0,
}

/// How an Event ACCUMULATES — a per-Event property, chosen at creation and
/// fixed for its life (§2.4).
///
/// It is fixed rather than settable because the accumulated word means two
/// different things under the two modes, and a mode change with a non-zero word
/// would have to either reinterpret it or discard it. Creating a second Event
/// costs one slab slot.
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the register the mode arrives in, for the
/// reason `SystemOp` gives: the value is PROCESS-CONTROLLED, so a narrower
/// backing would need a truncation before `from(raw:)` and `0x100` would arrive
/// as a valid `Or`.
public enum EventMode: UInt {
    case Or = 0,
    case SaturatingSum = 1,
}

// =============================================================================
// The wait record (spec §2.2, as amended by design 178 M2 unit 3 rider 3)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Waiter.Wait` answers with a RECORD the kernel COPIES OUT into a buffer the
// caller supplies, not with registers. That is what lets the answer carry a
// per-waitable payload without the ABI growing a register per object kind: a
// Channel's readiness is not a Timer's is not an Event's, and a fixed register
// pair would have had to be the union of all of them forever.
//
// THE KEY IS UNIVERSAL AND IS FACTORED OUT. Every waitable answers with the
// word its attacher supplied (§2.2: it can directly encode the waiting task's
// identity, so the kernel neither interprets it nor constrains it); only the
// PAYLOAD varies by kind. So the record is `key, tag, payload...` and a reader
// gets the key without knowing what fired.
//
// THE WIRE FORMAT IS PUBLIC AND FIXED-WIDTH-BY-WORD: word 0 the key, word 1 the
// tag, word 2 onward the payload. It is machine words rather than fixed 32-bit
// fields because it never leaves the machine — unlike `sosimg`, which is a file
// two profiles must both read (design 47's discipline applies there and not
// here), this crosses one privilege boundary inside one address space.

/// Which kind of waitable a record describes — the record's TAG WORD.
///
/// Raw-backed (design 145's wire idiom): every case states its number, so a
/// reorder cannot renumber them, and the userspace decode reads it through
/// `from(raw:)`, which is total. The tag SPACE is extensible: §2.2 and §8
/// already name the kinds that follow (a Channel readable or reply-ready, a
/// Timer fired, an Interrupt asserted, a Thread or Process exited), and each
/// takes the next number without moving this one.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG IS A PANIC in the decode, not a `None` a caller handles. The
/// kernel WROTE it, out of this same declaration — so a tag userspace cannot
/// name is a kernel/`sos` skew, which is a bug in the pair rather than data
/// from an untrusted source. Never-hide says it should be loud.
public enum WaitTag: UInt {
    case Event = 0,
    case Interrupt = 1,
    case Timer = 2,
}

/// Words in a wait record: key, tag, payload.
public static WAIT_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static WAIT_KEY_WORD: Int = 0
public static WAIT_TAG_WORD: Int = 1
public static WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a wait record on THIS machine — the minimum capacity a caller must
/// give `Waiter.Wait`, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static`, which is a language limitation rather than a
/// choice: `sizeof<UInt>()` does not fold in a static initializer, even though
/// design 186's own diagnostic lists it among the things that do (DF-178f). The
/// arithmetic folds perfectly well in an ordinary expression, so the constant is
/// spelled where it is used and this is the one place it is written.
public func wait_record_bytes() -> Int {
    WAIT_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// What became ready, decoded — one case per waitable kind.
///
/// The Event's payload is its ACCUMULATED WORD, and carrying it here is what
/// makes the common wait one syscall instead of two: a dispatcher that only
/// needs to know how many times something happened never has to follow up with
/// a `receive`.
///
/// **THE DELIVERY CONSUMES THE WORD** (ruled Aug 17, user). This record is not a
/// copy left beside the object — the kernel reads the word and clears it in one
/// masked-interrupts step, so the bits in the record are bits nothing else will
/// ever be told about. A `receive` right after a wake therefore answers ZERO
/// unless somebody signalled in between, and a signal that lands after the take
/// re-arms readiness with its own bits alone.
///
/// TWO DOORS ONTO ONE VALUE, and that is the whole model: §2.4's `receive` is
/// the non-blocking poll and this record is the blocking one. A process picks
/// the door by whether it wants to park, never by what it will be told. The
/// alternative — a snapshot the wait leaves behind — would report the same bits
/// to the next wait as well, and a recipient cannot tell that duplicate from a
/// real second signal.
/// THE INTERRUPT'S PAYLOAD IS ITS LINE, and that is the whole answer a driver
/// needs: it woke because a device asserted, and the line says which. There is
/// no count and no timestamp, because §9 ratifies AT MOST ONE UNACKED FIRE per
/// Interrupt — a second assertion while the first is unacked cannot happen, the
/// line being masked — so "how many" is always one and would be a field that
/// only ever held the same number.
///
/// A driver that owns exactly one line already knows which it is, so the field
/// looks redundant there; it is not, because the shape §2.2 exists for is a
/// dispatcher parked on SEVERAL. Such a dispatcher gets the line without a
/// side-table, exactly as it gets the key.
/// THE TIMER'S PAYLOAD IS ITS FIRE COUNT, and it is a count where the
/// Interrupt's deliberately is not (design 232 unit 1). The two differ because
/// their invariants do: §9 masks a line on fire, so an Interrupt can never have
/// two outstanding and "how many" would always read one — while a Timer has no
/// mask and nothing to hold it back, so a periodic one whose consumer was
/// descheduled genuinely expired several times. COALESCING those into a number
/// is what stops a slow consumer from either falling permanently behind a queue
/// of fires or silently losing them.
///
/// It SATURATES rather than wrapping, on §2.4's counting-Event reasoning
/// exactly: zero is what "not ready" means, so a count that rolled through it
/// would make a busy timer go quiet.
///
/// THE WAIT CONSUMES IT, exactly as it consumes an Event's word: the count is
/// drained by the wait that reports it, because a Timer has no second op to
/// drain with and needs none — the fire was delivered to somebody, so it is
/// spent. That is the ack-free half of the timerfd model. The Interrupt is the
/// kind that differs, and §9 says why: its readiness ends at the driver's `ack`,
/// because the line stays masked until the DEVICE has been serviced.
public enum WaitPayload {
    case Event(word: UInt),
    case Interrupt(line: UInt),
    case Timer(fires: UInt),
}

/// `Waiter.Wait`'s answer: the attacher's key, and what fired.
public struct WaitResult {
    /// The word supplied at `add`, handed back unread.
    public key: UInt,
    public what: WaitPayload,
}

// =============================================================================
// The Clock and Timer records (spec §2's Clock/Timer rows; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO MORE RECORDS, AND ONE OF THEM GOES THE OTHER WAY. `Clock.Now` answers
// with a copy-OUT record on the `Waiter.Wait` precedent; `Timer.Arm` takes its
// arguments through a copy-IN record, which is the funnel's mirror twin and is
// built here because M4's Channel `send` needs exactly this shape for a message
// body (§2.1). Timer.Arm is its small first consumer precisely as Waiter.Wait
// was for copy-out.
//
// WHY EITHER NEEDS A RECORD AT ALL: a NANOSECOND COUNT IS 64 BITS. Anything
// narrower wraps in four seconds, which is not a clock. One of the two profiles
// has 32-bit registers, so one time is two words and `arm`'s pair is four —
// past the three argument registers §5.7 gives an op, and past what a single
// value register can answer with.
//
// THESE RECORDS ARE INDEXED IN DOUBLEWORDS, NOT MACHINE WORDS, and that is the
// one way they differ from the wait record above. A wait record's fields are a
// KEY, a TAG and a PAYLOAD — all of them machine-word-shaped things (a task
// identity, a line number, an accumulated word), so it follows the register.
// These fields are TIMES, and a time is 64 bits on every machine SOS will ever
// run on, so following the register would make the layout differ per profile
// for no reason. Both still stay inside one address space, so design 47's
// wire-format discipline still does not apply — what applies is that the field
// width is a property of the QUANTITY rather than of the machine.

/// Doublewords in a `Clock.Now` answer: the reading, and nothing else.
public static CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 1

/// Where the reading sits. Named rather than written, on both sides.
public static CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT: Int = 0

/// Bytes in a `Clock.Now` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f) and folds
/// perfectly well in an ordinary expression.
public func clock_now_record_bytes() -> Int {
    CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

/// Doublewords in a `Timer.Arm` request: the first deadline, then the interval.
public static TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each lives.
///
/// `AFTER` IS RELATIVE AND `INTERVAL` IS NOT A TIME AT ALL, which is the one
/// thing a reader has to carry away from this layout. The first field says how
/// long from NOW the first fire is — relative, because an absolute first
/// deadline would make every caller read the clock first and race between the
/// two syscalls. The second is a PERIOD, and zero means one-shot: a timer that
/// fires once and disarms itself.
public static TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT: Int = 0
public static TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `Timer.Arm` record — exactly what the caller must hand over, and
/// what the kernel checks the length against.
public func timer_arm_record_bytes() -> Int {
    TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

// =============================================================================
// Status words (spec §5.7)
// =============================================================================

/// The status a syscall returns in the status register.
///
/// **A STATUS IS FOR WHAT THE CALLER COULD NOT HAVE KNOWN** (ratified Aug 8,
/// carried into design 178's ruling Aug 15). A caller error the process could
/// have checked — an invalid handle, an op the object does not have, a right it
/// was not given, driving a thread that is not itself — TERMINATES the process
/// and is not in this enum at all. It is a `FaultReason` below. What is left
/// here is the class a correct process can still meet: resource exhaustion, and
/// (when there are peers to die, which is M3) peer death.
///
/// That cut is why `BadHandle`, `BadOp` and `AccessDenied` are GONE from this
/// enum rather than merely unused. They moved to `FaultReason` keeping their
/// numbers, so the tags read the same in a kernel report and cannot be confused
/// with a status a process might branch on. Values 1-3 are left as a gap here
/// for exactly that reason: reusing them would make an old build's `BadHandle`
/// and a new build's status collide on the wire.
///
/// These tags ARE part of the contract in a way the op numbers are not, because
/// a process branches on them, so the backing is FIXED-WIDTH (design 47: a
/// value that crosses an ABI pins its width) even though the register carrying
/// it is wider.
///
/// A STATUS, NOT AN ERROR (ratified Aug 7): the type is named for what it is —
/// an enum with an `Ok` case describes how a call went, and only some of those
/// ways are failures. The `Sos` prefix says whose status it is, which is the
/// other half of the rename: the hosted runtime has its own frozen `SysError`
/// (errno tags, `sawc/rt/ABI.md`) and the two contracts are unrelated. They now
/// read as unrelated too.
///
/// `Ok` is a case because this enum is the wire table and 0 is in it. It is the
/// one value that is not a failure, and `sos`'s `checked` is the single place
/// that turns it into `Ok(())` — which is why an `Err(SosStatus.Ok)` cannot be
/// constructed anywhere downstream.
///
/// `Unknown` is NOT a status the kernel returns. It is where the userspace
/// mapping's `from(raw:)` miss lands, so a tag this build does not define stays
/// a typed error instead of being read as success. In M1 it is unreachable —
/// both halves compile from this one declaration — and it exists for the build
/// skew that static linking makes impossible today and a mapped vDSO will not.
public enum SosStatus: UInt8 {
    case Ok = 0,
    case NoResource = 4,
    case Unknown = 255,
}

public extension SosStatus {
    /// A short human-readable tag, for a diagnostic or a log line.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Ok -> "ok",
            case NoResource -> "out of kernel objects",
            case Unknown -> "unknown status",
        }
    }

    /// Whether this status reports a failure. The inverse of `Ok`, named so a
    /// caller reads intent rather than a comparison.
    public func is_failure(&self) -> Bool {
        match self {
            case Ok -> false,
            case _ -> true,
        }
    }
}

public extension SosStatus: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

public extension SosStatus: Error {}

// =============================================================================
// Fault reasons (design 178's faults ruling, ratified Aug 15)
// =============================================================================

/// Why the kernel TERMINATED a process.
///
/// The other half of the cut `SosStatus` describes: these are the caller errors
/// a process could have checked, and meeting one ends the process rather than
/// returning it a word to branch on. It is the same line the language draws at
/// an indexed accessor — an out-of-range index panics, it does not hand back a
/// flag — and it cuts userspace error handling down to the failures that are
/// real.
///
/// A process never RECEIVES one of these: it is dead by the time the tag
/// exists. It reaches a human through the kernel's report, and the harness
/// asserts on that line. The first three keep the numbers they had as statuses
/// so a reader of an old transcript and a new one sees the same tag for the
/// same mistake.
///
/// `UInt8` because it is a kernel-internal tag that crosses no register.
public enum FaultReason: UInt8 {
    case BadHandle = 1,
    case BadOp = 2,
    case AccessDenied = 3,
    case NotSelf = 4,
    case BadState = 5,
    case BadEntry = 6,
    case Deadlock = 7,
    case NotWaitable = 8,
    case BadArg = 9,
    case BadAlign = 10,
    case BadBuffer = 11,
    case DuplicateKey = 12,
    case LineBound = 13,
}

public extension FaultReason {
    /// The short tag the kernel's termination line carries.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case BadHandle -> "bad handle",
            case BadOp -> "bad op",
            case AccessDenied -> "access denied",
            case NotSelf -> "not the calling thread",
            // Not "thread in the wrong state" any more: the tag has covered
            // every object kind since Events and Waiters landed, and it covers
            // an Interrupt acked before it fired now.
            case BadState -> "object in the wrong state",
            case BadEntry -> "thread entry outside the process",
            case Deadlock -> "every thread blocked",
            case NotWaitable -> "not a waitable",
            case BadArg -> "argument outside its domain",
            case BadAlign -> "buffer is not word-aligned",
            // Direction-neutral since design 232 unit 1 opened the copy-IN
            // door beside the copy-out one. The two check DIFFERENT windows —
            // outbound must land in the process's writable memory, inbound
            // merely inside the memory it was granted — so a wording naming
            // one of them was accurate for exactly as long as there was one.
            case BadBuffer -> "buffer is outside the process's memory",
            case DuplicateKey -> "an attachment already uses that key",
            case LineBound -> "that interrupt line is already bound",
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Process status (spec §8)
// =============================================================================

/// How a process ended, or that it has not. Spec §8: ONE fixed-width status
/// word, KIND in the high bits and a code in the low bits, so a supervisor
/// reads both out of one register.
///
/// `Killed` is declared and unreachable in M2 for the reason `ProcessRight`
/// gives: there is no second process to be killed by. It is here because the
/// word's ENCODING is what a supervisor parses, and a kind added later would
/// renumber nothing but would still be a wire change.
public enum ProcessStatusKind: UInt8 {
    case Running = 0,
    case Exited = 1,
    case Faulted = 2,
    case Killed = 3,
}

/// Where the kind sits in the status word. The low half is the code — an exit
/// status, or a `FaultReason` — so both fit a 32-bit reader on either profile.
public static PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT: Int = 16
public static PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK: UInt = 0xFFFF

/// Assemble the §8 status word.
public func process_status_word(kind: ProcessStatusKind, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    ((((kind as UInt8) as UInt) << PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT)
     | (code & PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK))
}

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle set (spec §12)
// =============================================================================

/// The handle the kernel gives root for the System object. Root receives it in
/// the first argument register at entry rather than assuming it, so this
/// constant is the KERNEL's choice of what to mint — not a number userspace is
/// expected to know.
public static ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 1

/// The `stack_top` handed to a user entry that was granted no stack region.
public static NO_USER_STACK: UInt = 0
